<section class="section" id="classifier">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Step 2: BLS alone vs. a learned vetting step</p>
    <h2>Does a classifier agree with the search?</h2>
    <p>
      Trained a small CNN and a gradient-boosted model on real Kepler KOI dispositions (confirmed planets
      and false positives, not the pre-cleaned Kaggle set), then asked both to vet the 23 real detections
      from the validation systems above, where the catalog match already tells us which detections were
      real.
    </p>

    <div class="compare-row">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num accent">69.6<span class="stat-den">%</span></span>
        <span class="stat-label">CNN agreement with ground truth</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num muted">34.8<span class="stat-den">%</span></span>
        <span class="stat-label">Gradient-boosted agreement — worse than the 73.9% majority baseline</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="figure">
      <img
        src="/images/bls_vs_classifier_disagreements.png"
        alt="Three TRAPPIST-1 transits with a clean, visually obvious dip that the gradient-boosted model scored as unlikely"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 2. Three real TRAPPIST-1 planets, each showing a clean, symmetric, unambiguous transit, that
        the gradient-boosted baseline scored as unlikely to be real. The CNN's learned shape representation
        generalized better to these targets than a small hand-engineered feature set trained on a different
        random sample of KOIs. Reported as the interesting finding it is, not smoothed over.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .compare-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 64px;
    margin: 40px 0 48px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .stat-num.accent { color: var(--accent); }
  .stat-num.muted { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .compare-row .stat-label { max-width: 22ch; }
  .figure img {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .compare-row { gap: 32px; }
  }
</style>
